{{also|Point}}
==English==

===Alternative forms===
* {{alter|en|p'int}}

===Pronunciation===
* {{enPR|point|a=UK,GA}}, {{IPA|en|/pɔɪnt/|[pʰɔɪ̯nt]|[pʰɔɪ̯nt̚]}}
* {{audio|en|en-us-point.ogg|a=US}}
* {{audio|en|en-au-point.ogg|a=AU}}
* {{audio|en|LL-Q1860 (eng)-Vealhurl-point.wav|a=Southern England}}
* {{rhymes|en|ɔɪnt|s=1}}

===Etymology 1===
{{ety|en|id=noun|:inh|enm:poynt}}
From {{inh|en|enm|poynt}}, from {{der|en|fro|point|g=m||dot; minute amount}}, from {{der|en|la|pūnctum|t=a hole punched in; a point, puncture}}, substantive use of {{m|la|pūnctus|g=m}}, perfect passive participle of {{m|la|pungō|t=to prick, punch}}; alternatively, from {{der|en|fro|pointe|g=f||sharp tip}}, from {{der|en|la|pūnctus|pūncta|g=f|pos=past participle}}, all from {{der|en|itc-pro|*pungō|t=to sting, prick}}.

Mostly displaced native {{cog|enm|ord||point}}, from {{cog|ang|ord||point}}. {{doublet|en|pointe|ponto|puncto|punctum|punt<id:point>|punto}}.

====Noun====
{{wikidata lexeme|L3808}}
{{en-noun|~}}

# A [[small]] [[dot]] or [[mark]].
## Something tiny, as a pinprick; a very small mark. {{defdate|from 15th c.}}
##: {{ux|en|The stars showed as tiny '''points''' of yellow light.}}
## A [[full stop]] or other terminal punctuation mark. {{defdate|from 14th c.}}
##* {{quote-book|en|year=1735|title=The Prologue to the Satires|author=Alexander Pope|passage=Commas and '''points''' they set exactly right.}}
## {{lb|en|mathematics}} A [[decimal point]] (now especially when reading decimal fractions aloud). {{defdate|from 18th c.}}
##: {{ux|en|10.5 is "ten '''point''' five", or ten and a half.}}
##: {{ux|en|0.375 cm is nought '''point''' three seven five of a centimeter.}}
## Each of the [[mark]]s or [[stroke]]s written above [[letter]]s, especially in Semitic languages, to indicate [[vowel]]s, stress etc. {{defdate|from 17th c.}}
## {{lb|en|music}} A dot or mark used to designate certain tones or time. In ancient music, it distinguished or characterized certain tones or styles (points of perfection, of augmentation, etc.). In modern music, it is placed on the right of a note to raise its value, or prolong its time, by one half.
## {{lb|en|by extension}} A [[note]]; a [[tune]].
##* {{RQ:Scott Woodstock|passage=Sound the trumpet &mdash; not a levant, or a flourish, but a '''point''' of war.}}
## {{lb|en|mathematics|science}} A [[zero-dimensional]] [[mathematical]] [[object]] representing a location in one or more dimensions; something considered to have position but no magnitude or direction. {{defdate|from 14th c.}} {{C|en|Shapes}}
# A [[small]] [[discrete]] [[division]] or [[individual]] [[feature]] of something.
## An [[individual]] [[element]] in a [[larger]] [[whole]]; a particular [[detail]], [[thought]], or [[quality]]. {{defdate|from 13th c.}}
##: {{ux|en|The Congress debated the finer '''points''' of the bill.}}
## A particular [[moment]] in an [[event]] or occurrence; a [[juncture]]. {{defdate|from 13th c.}}
##: {{ux|en|There comes a '''point''' in a marathon when some people give up.}}
##: {{ux|en|At this '''point''' in the meeting, I'd like to propose a new item for the agenda.}}
## {{lb|en|archaic}} [[condition|Condition]], [[state]]. {{defdate|from 13th c.}}
##: {{ux|en|She was not feeling in good '''point'''.}}
## A [[topic]] of [[discussion]] or [[debate]]; a [[proposition]]; a [[count]]. {{defdate|from 14th c.}}
##: {{ux|en|I made the '''point''' that we all had an interest to protect.}}
## {{lb|en|US|slang|dated}} An item of private information; a hint; a tip; a pointer.
## {{senseid|en|focus of attention, main idea}} A [[focus]] of [[conversation]] or [[consideration]]; the [[main]] [[idea]].
##: {{ux|en|The '''point''' is that we should stay together, whatever happens.}}
## {{senseid|en|purpose or objective}} A [[purpose]] or [[objective]], which makes something [[meaningful]]. {{defdate|from 14th c.}}
##: {{ant|en|pointlessness|uselessness|purposelessness|meaninglessness}}<!--in order of semantic proximity-->
##: {{nearsyn|en|meaning#Noun|meaningfulness|use<id:usefulness or purpose>}}
##: {{ux|en|Since the decision has already been made, I see little '''point''' in further discussion.}}
##* {{RQ:Lincoln Pratt's Patients|chapter=4|passage=I told him about everything I could think of; and what I couldn't think of he did. He asked about six questions during my yarn, but every question had a '''point''' to it. At the end he bowed and thanked me once more. As a thanker he was main-truck high; I never see anybody so polite.}}
##* {{quote-song|en|author={{w|Genesis (band)|Genesis}}|authorlabel=no|title={{w|That's All (Genesis song)|That's All}}|album={{w|Genesis (Genesis album)|Genesis}}|date=31 October 1983|url=https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Vzyn60Zns-E|text=But I love you / More than I wanted to / There's no '''point''' in trying to pretend}}
##* {{quote-song|en|year=2023|artist=w:Coach Party|album={{w|Killjoy (Coach Party album)|Killjoy}}|title=What's the Point in Life|passage=We're all gonna die<br>What's the '''point''' in life<br>What's the '''point''' in life if we all die?}}
## {{lb|en|obsolete}} The smallest quantity of something; a [[jot]], a [[whit]]. {{defdate|14th|17th c.}}
##* {{quote-book|en|year=1590|author=Edmund Spenser|title=The Faerie Queene|section=I.ii|passage=full large of limbe and euery ioint / He was, and cared not for God or man a '''point'''.}}
## {{lb|en|obsolete}} A tiny amount of time; a [[moment]]. {{defdate|14th|17th c.}}
##* {{quote-book|en|year=1599|author={{w|John Davies (poet)|John Davies}}|title=Nosce Teipsum|chapter=Of the Soule of Man and the Immortalitie Thereof|passage=When time's first '''point''' begun / Made he all souls.}}
## A specific [[location]] or place, seen as a spatial position. {{defdate|from 14th c.}}
##: {{ux|en|We should meet at a pre-arranged '''point'''.}}
## A distinguishing [[quality]] or [[characteristic]]. {{defdate|from 15th c.}}
##: {{ux|en|Logic isn't my strong '''point'''.}}
## {{lb|en|in the plural|dated}} The chief or excellent features.
##: {{ux|en|the '''points''' of a horse}}
##* {{quote-book|en|year=1931|author=Arnold Bennett|title=The Night Visitor and Other Stories|page=290
|passage=Knowledge was always useful, and he had frequently heard the words 'Great Portland Street' on the lips of his son, who regularly perused all the twelve automobilistic papers, and who was apparently the most learned pundit and inclusive encyclopædia ever created on the subject of petrol-driven vehicles, their prices, and their innumerable '''points'''.}}
## {{senseid|en|fur}}{{lb|en|usually|in the plural}} An area of contrasting [[colour]] on an [[animal]], especially a dog; a marking. {{defdate|from 19th c.}}
##: {{ux|en|The '''point''' color of that cat was a deep, rich sable.}}
## {{lb|en|now only in phrases}} A [[tenth]]; formerly also a [[twelfth]]. {{defdate|from 17th c.}}
##: {{ux|en|Possession is nine '''points''' of the law.}}
## {{ellipsis of|en|percentage point}}.
##* {{quote-book|en|year=2013|author=Louise Levison|title=Filmmakers and Financing: Business Plans for Independents|page=67
|passage=We have yet to touch on the idea of stars and directors receiving gross '''points''', which is a percentage of the studio's gross dollar (e.g., the $5.00 studio share of the total box office dollar in Table 4.1). Even if the '''points''' are paid on "first dollar," the reference is only to studio share.}}
## {{senseid|en|unit of scoring}}{{lb|en|sports|video games|board games}} A unit of [[scoring]] in a [[game]] or [[competition]]. {{defdate|from 18th c.}}
##: {{ux|en|The one with the most '''points''' will win the game.}}
## {{lb|en|video games|board games}} A unit of various [[numerical]] [[parameter]]s used in a game, e.g. [[health]], [[experience]], [[stamina]], [[mana]].
##: {{ux|en|This attack deals 320 '''points''' of damage.}}
##: {{ux|en|Defeating the boss grants 60 experience '''points'''.}}
## {{lb|en|economics}} A unit used to express differences in prices of [[stock]]s and [[share]]s. {{defdate|from 19th c.}}
## {{lb|en|typography}} A unit of measure equal to 1/12 of a [[pica]], or approximately 1/72 of an [[inch]] (exactly 1/72 of an inch in the digital era). {{defdate|from 19th c.}}
## {{lb|en|UK}} An electric [[power]] socket. {{defdate|from 20th c.}}
## {{lb|en|UK|AU|NZ|obsolete}} A [[unit of measure]] for [[rain]], equal to 0.254 mm or 0.01 of an [[inch]].
## {{lb|en|automotive|mostly|in the plural}} Either of the two metal surfaces in a [[distributor]] which close or open to allow or prevent the flow of current through the [[ignition coil]]. There is usually a moving point, pushed by the distributor cam, and a fixed point, and they are built together as a unit.
# A [[sharp]] [[extremity]].
## The sharp [[tip]] of an [[object]]. {{defdate|from 14th c.}}
##: {{ux|en|Cut the skin with the '''point''' of the knife.}}
##* {{RQ:Marlowe Tamburlaine|part=1|scene=iii|page=50|passage=Leaue words & let them feele your lances '''pointes'''}}
## Any projecting extremity of an object. {{defdate|from 14th c.}}
## An object which has a sharp or tapering tip. {{defdate|from 14th c.}}
##: {{ux|en|His cowboy belt was studded with '''points'''.}}
### {{lb|en|archaeology}} A spearhead or similar object hafted to a handle.
###* {{quote-book|en|year=2018|author=w:Tim Flannery|title=Europe: A Natural History|page=|passage=Solutrean '''points''' resemble the canines of the sabre-toothed cats.}}
### {{lb|en|medicine|obsolete}} A [[vaccine point]].
## {{lb|en|backgammon}} Each of the [[twelve]] [[triangular]] positions in either [[table]] of a [[backgammon]] [[board]], on which the [[stone]]s are played. {{defdate|from 15th c.}}
## A [[peninsula]] or [[promontory]]. {{defdate|from 15th c.}}
## The position at the front or vanguard of an advancing force. {{defdate|from 16th c.}}
##* {{RQ:Torgoff Can't Find My Way Home| page=189| passage=Willie Jones decided to become Kimani Jones, Black Panther, on the day his best friend, Otis Nicholson, stepped on a mine while walking '''point''' during a sweep in the central highlands.}}
### {{lb|en|by extension}} An operational or public leadership position in a risky endeavor.
###* {{quote-book|en|title=Music of the Soul| volume=1| author=Erik Schubach| year=2013| passage="When do we pull the trigger?" he asked. I was quick to respond, "If Tammy get's Mrs. Wellington to agree, she'll call you in a couple hours. Then just pull out all stops. Tammy has '''point''' on this, I don't want to hear from you unless it's an all clear."}}
###* {{RQ:WaPo|title=Trump’s economic agenda is unfocused. Here’s how to fix it.| author=Paul Winfree| date=July 2, 2018| passage=The president’s senior policy adviser, Stephen Miller, has been '''point''' on immigration policy.}}
###* {{quote-journal|en|passage=He captained Regis High School’s 1958 squad, but now runs '''point''' on infectious diseases.| title=Dr. {{w|Anthony Fauci}} throws first pitch at Nationals-Yankees MLB opener| author=Gabe Lacques| journal=USA TODAY| date=July 23, 2020}}
###* {{quote-journal|en|journal=Pikes Peak Courier|passage=Instead of one '''point'''-person taking all the parents’ questions, WPSD has “put together coaches and ambassadors to handle calls so one person doesn’t have to handle 2,500 calls,” Woolf said.|title=Woodland Park School District plans for Aug. 25 reopening with multiple learning options|author=Michelle Karas|date=August 11, 2020}}
### {{ellipsis of|en|point man}}.
## Each of the main directions on a [[compass]], usually considered to be 32 in number; a direction. {{defdate|from 16th c.}}
## {{lb|en|navigation|nautical}} The difference between two points of the compass; a [[unit]] of [[bearing]] equal to one thirty-second of a [[circle]], ''i.e.'' 11.25°.
##: {{ux|en|to fall off a '''point'''}}
##: {{ux|en|Ship ahoy, three '''points''' off the starboard bow!}}
## [[pointedness|Pointedness]] of speech or writing; a penetrating or decisive quality of [[expression]]. {{defdate|from 17th c.}}
##* {{RQ:James Maisie|passage=There was moreover a hint of the duchess in the infinite '''point''' with which, as she felt, she exclaimed: "And this is what you call coming ''often''?"}}
##* {{RQ:Bramah Bravo of London|passage=And with an emphatic nod to give '''point''' to her self-possession Mrs. Larch vanished.}}
## {{lb|en|railroads|UK|in the plural}} A [[railroad switch]]. {{defdate|from 19th c.}}
## A [[tine]] or [[snag]] of an [[antler]].
## {{lb|en|heraldry}} One of the "corners" of the [[escutcheon]]: the [[base]] (bottom center) unless a qualifier is added (''point dexter'', ''point dexter base'', ''point sinister'', ''point sinister base''), generally when separately tinctured. {{q|Compare {{m|en|terrace}}, {{m|en|champaine|point champaine}}, {{m|en|enté en point}}.}} [[File:Type pièce Vetement.svg|right|120px|thumb|This is sometimes blazoned ''argent, four points gules''; otherwise, it is {{l|fr|vêtu}}.]]
## {{lb|en|heraldry|by extension}} An ordinary similar to a [[pile]] (but sometimes shorter), extending upward from the [[base]]. {{q|Often termed a ''point pointed''.}}
##* {{quote-book|en|year=1828|author=William Berry|title=Encyclopaedia Heraldica Or Complete Dictionary of Heraldry
|text=The '''point''', according to Edmondson, (meaning the poin<!--preceding sense-->t pointed,) is an ordinary somewhat resembling the pile, issuing from the base, as in Plate VII. fig. 24, and is sometimes termed a base point pointed, but the word base is superfluous, as that is the proper place of the point;{{nb...}}}}
# The act of [[pointing]].
## The act of pointing, as of the [[foot]] [[downward]] in certain [[dance]] positions.
## The gesture of extending the index finger in a direction in order to indicate something.
##* {{quote-book|en|year=2005|author=Marc Marschark; Patricia Elizabeth Spencer|title=Oxford handbook of deaf studies, language, and education
|passage={{...}} DCDP children are exposed to more '''points''' and gesturelike signs in their linguistic environment {{...}}}}
## The attitude assumed by a [[pointer]] [[dog]] when he finds [[game]].
##: {{ux|en|The dog came to a '''point'''.}}
## {{lb|en|falconry}} The [[perpendicular]] rising of a [[hawk]] over the place where its [[prey]] has gone into cover.
## {{lb|en|fencing}} A [[movement]] executed with the [[sabre]] or [[foil]].
##: {{ux|en|tierce '''point'''}}
# {{lb|en|nautical}} A short piece of [[cordage]] used in [[reef]]ing [[sail]]s.
# {{lb|en|historical}} A string or lace used to tie together certain garments.
#* {{RQ:Scott Fortunes of Nigel|passage= pick your purse while they tie your '''points''', and cut your throat while they smooth your pillow}}
# Lace worked by the needle.
#: {{ux|en|'''point''' de Venise; Brussels '''point'''}}
#* {{RQ:Massinger Maid of Honour|scene=i|sig=&#91;K4&#93;|page=82|passage=And I to make all knovv, I am not ſhallovv, / VVill have my '''points''' of [[cochineal#Noun|Cucchineale]] and yellovv.}}
#* {{RQ:Landon Francesca Carrara|volume=I|page=297|passage=He wore a garb rather fanciful, of a silver-grey colour, trimmed with crimson, and a narrow edging of silver; the lace round his throat was of the finest '''point'''; {{...}}}}
# In various sports, a position of a certain player, or, by extension, the player occupying that position.
## {{lb|en|cricket|countable|uncountable}} A [[fielding]] [[position]] [[square]] of the [[wicket]] on the [[off side]], between [[gully]] and [[cover]]. {{defdate|from 19th c.}}
## {{lb|en|lacrosse|ice hockey|countable|uncountable}} The position of the player of each side who stands a short distance in front of the [[goalkeeper]].
## {{lb|en|baseball|countable|uncountable}} The position of the [[pitcher]] and [[catcher]].
## {{lb|en|hunting}} A spot to which a straight run is made; hence, a straight run from point to point; a cross-country run.

