===Etymology 2===
{{ety|en|id=verb|:inh|enm:pointen}}
From {{inh|en|enm|pointen}}, {{m|enm|poynten}}, from {{der|en|fro|pointier}}, {{m|fro|pointer}}, {{m|fro|poynter}},<ref>{{R:MED Online|pointen|v.(1)|MED33867|20 January 2020|date=2018}}</ref> from {{m|fro|point}} from {{der|en|la|pūnctum}}.

====Verb====
{{en-verb}}

# {{lb|en|intransitive}} To extend the [[index finger]] in the direction of something in order to show where it is or to draw attention to it.
#: {{ux|en|It's rude to '''point''' at other people.}}
#* {{RQ:Shakespeare Taming of the Shrew|3|2|passage=Now must the world '''point''' at poor Katharine.}}
#* {{RQ:Dryden Juvenal Satires|passage='''Point''' at the tattered coat and ragged shoe.}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|date=October 23, 2011|author=Becky Ashton|work=BBC Sport
|title=QPR 1 - 0 Chelsea
|url=http://news.bbc.co.uk/sport2/hi/football/eng_prem/15325522.stm
|passage=Luiz struggled with the movement of Helguson in the box, as he collected a long ball and the Spaniard barged him over, leaving referee Chris Foy little option but to '''point''' to the spot.}}
# {{lb|en|intransitive}} To draw attention to something or indicate a direction.
#: {{ux|en|The arrow of a compass '''points''' north}}
#: {{ux|en|The skis were '''pointing''' uphill.}}
#: {{ux|en|The arrow on the map '''points''' towards the entrance}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|date=2013-06-07|author=[http://www.guardian.co.uk/profile/edpilkington Ed Pilkington]
|volume=188|issue=26|page=6|magazine=w:The Guardian Weekly
|title=&lsquo;Killer robots&rsquo; should be banned in advance, UN told
|url=http://www.guardian.co.uk/science/2013/may/29/killer-robots-ban-un-warning
|passage=In his submission to the UN, [Christof] Heyns '''points''' to the experience of drones. Unmanned aerial vehicles were intended initially only for surveillance, and their use for offensive purposes was prohibited, yet once strategists realised their perceived advantages as a means of carrying out targeted killings, all objections were swept out of the way.}}
# {{lb|en|intransitive}} To face in a particular direction.
# {{lb|en|transitive|sometimes|figurative}} To direct toward an object; to aim.
#: {{ux|en|to '''point''' a gun at a wolf, or a cannon at a fort}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1853|author=Great Britain. Parliament. House of Commons|title=Parliamentary Papers|volume=11|page=267
|passage=Mr. Fitzsimons '''pointed''' my attention to an outside car on which was written, "Take warning," or something of that kind, and he pointed that out to me, and drew my attention to it, as a thing likely to intimidate {{...}}}}
# To give a point to; to sharpen; to cut, forge, grind, or file to an acute end.
#: {{ux|en|to '''point''' a dart, a pencil, or (figuratively) a moral}}
# {{lb|en|intransitive}} To [[indicate]] a probability of something.
#* {{RQ:Guardian|date=December 21, 2011|author=Helen Pidd
|title=Europeans migrate south as continent drifts deeper into crisis
|url=http://www.guardian.co.uk/world/2011/dec/21/europe-migrants-crisis-irish-portuguese
|passage=Tens of thousands of Portuguese, Greek and Irish people have left their homelands this year, many heading for the southern hemisphere. Anecdotal evidence '''points''' to the same happening in Spain and Italy.}}
# {{lb|en|ambitransitive|masonry}} To repair [[mortar]].
# {{lb|en|transitive|masonry}} To fill up and finish the joints of (a wall), by introducing additional cement or mortar, and bringing it to a smooth surface.
# {{lb|en|stone-cutting}} To cut, as a surface, with a pointed tool.
#* {{quote-journal|en|date=2020 May 20|author=Philip Haigh|title=Ribblehead: at the heart of the S&C's survival and its revival: Ribblehead Viaduct repairs|journal=Rail|page=27|text=Damaged stone will be removed, and the new stone installed and '''pointed''' to ensure a comprehensive match to maintain the integrity of the structure.}}
# {{lb|en|transitive}} To [[direct]] or [[encourage]] (someone) in a particular direction.
#: {{ux|en|If he asks for food, '''point''' him toward the refrigerator.}}
#* {{RQ:Homer Pope Iliad|passage=Whosoever should be guided through his battles by Minerva, and '''pointed''' to every scene of them.}}
# {{lb|en|transitive|mathematics}} To separate an integer from a decimal with a [[decimal point]].
# {{lb|en|transitive}} To mark with [[diacritics]].
# {{lb|en|transitive|music}} To mark (e.g. an Anglican psalm) with [[pointing]].
# {{lb|en|dated}} To supply with [[punctuation mark]]s; to [[punctuate]].
#: {{ux|en|to '''point''' a composition}}
# {{lb|en|transitive|computing}} To direct the [[central processing unit]] to seek information at a certain location in [[memory]].
# {{lb|en|transitive|Internet}} To direct requests sent to a [[domain name]] to the [[IP address]] corresponding to that domain name.
# {{lb|en|intransitive|nautical}} To sail close to the wind.
#: {{ux|en|Bear off a little, we're '''pointing'''.}}
# {{lb|en|intransitive|hunting}} To indicate the presence of [[game]] by a fixed and steady look, as certain hunting dogs do.
#* {{RQ:John Gay Rural Sports|edition=2nd|page=16|passage=Novv the vvarm Scent aſſures the Covey near, / He {{quote-gloss|the dog}} treads vvith Caution, and he '''points''' vvith Fear; {{...}}}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1850|author=w:Thomas Keightley|title=The Fairy Mythology|location=London|publisher=H.G. Bohn|page=310|passage=The writer from whom we derive the following legends tells us that he knew an old man whose dog had '''''pointed''''' a troop of fairies, and though he could not see them he plainly heard their music sounding like a fiddle and a ''very small'' pair of pipes.}}
#* {{RQ:Bramah Bravo of London|passage=He says he found him, not a hour ago, under a clump in Birling Wood, because his own dog knew there was something there and as good as said so. I thought it sounded queer too, mum, but Mr. Batts did say that his dog stood and '''pointed''' at the bush and there when he looked was Nipper.}}
# {{lb|en|medicine|of an [[abscess]]}} To approximate to the [[surface]]; to [[head]].
# {{lb|en|dated}} To give [[point]] to (something said or done); to give particular prominence or force to.
#* {{RQ:Dickens Bleak House|passage=He '''points''' it, however, by no deviation from his straightforward manner of speech.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1924|author=w:EM Forster|title=A Passage to India|page=85|publisher=Penguin|year_published=2005
|passage=‘Oh, it is the great defect in our Indian character!’ – and, as if to '''point''' his criticism, the lights of the Civil Station appeared on a rise to the right.}}

